# TypeScript

`tsconfig.json` has `strict: true`. Still guard empty photo, missing price, etc. on the backend boundary. Do not turn `strict` off as a drive-by change.

## Core rules

- Do not add new `any`. Existing `any` on the backend boundary is tolerated; do not globally rewrite it.
- Prefer `unknown` + narrowing over `any`.
- Avoid `@ts-ignore` / `@ts-expect-error` unless unavoidable and commented why.
- Prefer `type` for data shapes; `interface` when you need `extends` or declaration merging.
- Domain types live in the module (`model/` or `api/types.ts`), not inside a UI component.
- Prettier: single quotes (including JSX), semicolons, `printWidth: 80`.

## Backend boundary

- Parse/normalize API responses in the module `api/` layer before they reach components.
- Model optional backend fields explicitly: `photo: string | null`, `price: Price | null`.
- Use discriminated unions for async UI state when it clarifies rendering.

## Functions and generics

- Prefer explicit return types on exported module helpers and API mappers.
- Do not use non-null assertion (`!`) to silence missing backend data — handle `null`/`undefined`.
- Avoid widening literals accidentally; use `as const` for fixed sets of statuses/tabs.
- Reuse existing domain types; do not duplicate the same shape under a new name.

---

## Examples

### 1. Nullable backend fields

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — assumes backend always sends a photo
function ExchangeProductCard({ product }: { product: { photo: string } }) {
  return <Image src={product.photo} alt="" />;
}

// ✅ GOOD
type ExchangeProduct = {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  photo: string | null;
};

function ExchangeProductCard({ product }: { product: ExchangeProduct }) {
  if (product.photo == null) return <ProductPhotoPlaceholder />;
  return <Image src={product.photo} alt={product.title} />;
}
```

### 2. unknown instead of any

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
function mapExchangeError(error: any) {
  return error.response.data.message;
}

// ✅ GOOD
function mapExchangeError(error: unknown): string {
  if (!isAxiosError(error)) return 'unknown_error';
  const message = error.response?.data?.message;
  return typeof message === 'string' ? message : 'unknown_error';
}
```

### 3. Discriminated async state

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — multiple booleans can contradict each other
type ExchangeDetailsState = {
  isLoading: boolean;
  isError: boolean;
  exchange: ExchangeDetails | null;
};

// ✅ GOOD
type ExchangeDetailsState =
  | { status: 'loading' }
  | { status: 'error'; message: string }
  | { status: 'success'; exchange: ExchangeDetails };
```

### 4. Domain types outside components

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — type declared inside component file
function CancelExchangeModal() {
  type Payload = { reason: string; comment?: string };
}

// ✅ GOOD — src/modules/exchange/model/exchange-types.ts
export type CancelExchangePayload = {
  reason: ExchangeCancelReason;
  comment?: string;
};
```

### 5. Avoid unsafe assertions

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
const status = response.data.status as ExchangeStatus;
const price = product.price!.amount;

// ✅ GOOD
function isExchangeStatus(value: string): value is ExchangeStatus {
  return EXCHANGE_STATUSES.includes(value as ExchangeStatus);
}

const status = isExchangeStatus(response.data.status)
  ? response.data.status
  : 'unknown';

const formattedPrice =
  product.price == null ? '' : String(product.price.amount);
```
